{"type":"standard","title":"Phase (waves)","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Phase (waves)</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q185553","titles":{"canonical":"Phase_(waves)","normalized":"Phase (waves)","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Phase (waves)</span></span>"},"pageid":24047,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Oscillating_sine_wave.gif/330px-Oscillating_sine_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":287},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Oscillating_sine_wave.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","width":520,"height":452},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1361007305","tid":"2c10b3fc-7037-11f1-95ec-6948c09e188e","timestamp":"2026-06-25T01:42:49Z","description":"Elapsed fraction of a cycle of a periodic function","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_(waves)","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_(waves)?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_(waves)?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Phase_(waves)"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_(waves)","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Phase_(waves)","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_(waves)?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Phase_(waves)"}},"extract":"In physics and mathematics, the phase of a wave or other periodic function  of some real variable  is an angle-like quantity representing the fraction of the cycle covered up to . It is expressed in such a scale that it varies by one full turn as the variable  goes through each period. It may be measured in any angular unit such as degrees or radians, thus increasing by 360° or  as the variable  completes a full period.","extract_html":"<p>In physics and mathematics, the <b>phase</b> of a wave or other periodic function <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/545fd099af8541605f7ee55f08225526be88ce57\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.741ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> of some real variable <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/65658b7b223af9e1acc877d848888ecdb4466560\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:0.84ex;height:2.009ex\" /></span> is an angle-like quantity representing the fraction of the cycle covered up to <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/65658b7b223af9e1acc877d848888ecdb4466560\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:0.84ex;height:2.009ex\" /></span>. It is expressed in such a scale that it varies by one full turn as the variable <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/65658b7b223af9e1acc877d848888ecdb4466560\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:0.84ex;height:2.009ex\" /></span> goes through each period. It may be measured in any angular unit such as degrees or radians, thus increasing by 360° or <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/73efd1f6493490b058097060a572606d2c550a06\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:2.494ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> as the variable <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/65658b7b223af9e1acc877d848888ecdb4466560\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:0.84ex;height:2.009ex\" /></span> completes a full period.</p>"}